WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:05.520
If the first Pacific Rim made us all feel
like giddy fifth graders, Pacific Rim: Uprising

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.380
makes us feel like giddy fifth graders just
wrote the movie.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:14.490
Steven S. DeKnight's sequel plays like a $150
million after-school cartoon — a city-leveling

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:17.060
monster mash with Rock 'Em Sock 'Em Robots.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:19.919
And there's no world in which that's a truly
bad thing.

00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:24.410
Of course, the humans win the day, fighting
back the Kaiju threat by learning the value

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:25.579
of teamwork.

00:00:25.579 --> 00:00:29.419
Even Ms. Shao sets aside her corporate greed
to assist the Jaeger pilots with their mission

00:00:29.419 --> 00:00:31.750
to stop the Kaiju assault on Mt. Fuji.

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:33.590
But was it all so clear-cut?

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:37.510
Let's clear up those pesky little details
that you may have missed in the final act.

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:40.770
Here's the ending of Pacific Rim: Uprising
explained.

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:42.870
Spoilers ahead, of course.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.060
Crazy Newt

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:48.440
Newton Geizsler, resident Kaiju biological
expert, was one of the best characters in

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:49.530
the first film.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:53.450
A little crazy, a little genius, he was the
one who finally put all the pieces together

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:56.780
so the Jaeger pilots knew how to close the
breach once and for all.

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:58.050
How'd he do it?

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:00.150
Drifting with a chunk of Kaiju brain.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.510
"And I'd like you to know it's all your fault.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.390
It really is, you drove me to this.

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:06.570
In which case ha I also won.

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:08.530
sort of."

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:11.030
Clearly, it made him a little crazy.

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:13.770
When Newton's first introduced in Pacific
Rim: Uprising, you just think he's kind of

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:17.159
a jerk because all that success went to his
head.

00:01:17.159 --> 00:01:20.930
Eventually, though, it's revealed that Newt's
private drift sessions with the Kaiju hive

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:23.969
mind have turned him into a lunatic brainwashed
traitor.

00:01:23.969 --> 00:01:29.639
And from that point on, all the chaos happening
in Tokyo is being controlled by him…or is

00:01:29.639 --> 00:01:30.639
it?

00:01:30.639 --> 00:01:34.280
Right before the mega-Kaiju leaves Tokyo,
it approaches the building where Newt's watching

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:38.600
the show and gives him a disapproving look,
like that Kaiju beast kind of wants him dead

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:39.600
too.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:43.159
Because even though Newt made it possible
for the Kaiju to come back, in the end he

00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:45.380
was just a pawn for their schemes.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:46.700
Kaiju centipede

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:51.270
After the breaches are shut down, the Pan-Pacific
Defense Corps realize that three Kaiju made

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:52.270
it to Earth.

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:56.289
These three baddies all converge on Tokyo,
where the remaining Jaegers take a stand to

00:01:56.289 --> 00:01:57.490
protect the city.

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:02.150
In the midst of all this city saving, Newt
releases his swarm of micro drones that bond

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:07.779
the three Kaiju into one massive Mechagodzilla
Kaiju, stitching them together like an interdimensional

00:02:07.779 --> 00:02:09.080
human centipede.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.860
If you blinked, you may have missed the transformation.

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:16.120
One second there are three big lizards, the
next there's only one bigger, more menacing

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:17.920
one.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:23.170
The film later shows a hologram of the creature
with its three brains — one for each adjoining

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:26.640
Kaiju — lined up inside the big beast's
body.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:27.970
Kamikaze strike

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:32.560
So this massive Kaiju-tron is heading for
Mt. Fuji, because falling into a volcano is

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:37.420
the best way for the Precursors — the alien
species that created the Kaiju — to terraform

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.450
the Earth to suit their needs.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:44.070
Since the monster has three brains, it also
has three weak spots, and the regular attacks

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:45.070
aren't going to work.

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:48.290
It'll take too long to destroy one brain,
let alone three of them.

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:50.620
This is no time for sparkle whips.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:55.730
With no other option, Jake Pentecost and Amara
launch their Yaeger almost into space, planning

00:02:55.730 --> 00:03:00.680
to drop it directly onto the Kaiju like Kirby's
Super Smash Bros. rock smash.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:04.260
"Game!"

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:06.970
Knucklehead

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:11.360
When things — whether they be spaceships,
meteorites, or robots — fall through Earth's

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:13.740
atmosphere from space, they get hot.

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:16.080
Like 3,000 degrees hot.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:20.660
So how did Jake and Amara survive their reentry
in a robot diving head-first with a broken

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:21.660
faceplate?

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.510
One word: knuckles.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:27.970
As they begin their descent, you can see the
Yaeger's knuckles turn red hot — it's shielding

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:29.410
their open cockpit.

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:33.060
Not only does their heroic dive look cool,
it's functional, too!

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.310
Would that have actually made a difference?

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:36.600
Yeah, probably not.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:40.420
"But nobody's expecting the giant robot movie
to be scientifically accurate.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:43.450
The point is, it was nice that they tried."

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:44.860
Pacific trilogy

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:49.350
In the pre-credits scene, we see Dr. Newton
Geiszler being held captive in a dark, gloomy

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:50.470
bunker somewhere.

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:54.780
He's still off his rocker, spouting nonsense
about how the overlords will find a way to

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:58.150
come back, and there's no way humans will
be able to stop them this time.

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:02.320
Viewers probably felt a minor stirring in
their bowels, picturing a third film in the

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:03.320
works.

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:06.780
In an interview with IGN, John Boyega, who
played Jake Pentecost and also served as a

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:10.490
producer on Uprising, reiterated the potential
for a third film, saying

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:13.180
"We'll probably have to go to their world
this time."

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:17.160
While the box office numbers will ultimately
be the deciding factor in whether or not we

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:23.070
get to see a third Pacific Rim film, we definitely
have our fleshy, non-Jaeger fingers crossed.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.320
Thanks for watching!

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:27.030
